{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_kvzwxqcpadbep5znhgoxdlyt7pqfks5g5u7ibcehsslwpe3ftr2q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four phases complete.\n\n## What the C program does\n\n```c\nint main() { int x = 0; scanf(\"%d\", &x); driver(x); return 0; }\n```\n\n`driver` memcpy's the 4 bytes of `x` into `char[4]`; `print_hex` prints each as `%02x` plus `\"\\n\"`. Since `scanf`'s return value is never checked, **all** branching lives inside the `%d` conversion: EOF, matching failure (leaves `x` at its initializer `0`), or success (glibc `strtol` → saturate in `long` → truncate to `int`).\n\n## Phase A — builds\n- C: `cd c_src/build && cmake .. && cmake --build .` → `c_src/build/driver`\n- Rust: `cd translation && cargo build --release` → `translation/target/release/driver`\n\nBoth built clean; the Rust crate had **no** compile errors.\n\n## Phases B & C — one real mismatch found and fixed\n\n**`SIGPIPE` disposition.** With stdout on a pipe whose reader had already closed:\n\n```\nC:    code=None,    signal=Some(13)   # killed by SIGPIPE\nRust: code=Some(0), signal=None       # exited normally\n```\n\nThe Rust stdlib installs `SIG_IGN` for `SIGPIPE` before `main`; a C program inherits `SIG_DFL`. The `EPIPE` was being swallowed by the existing `let _ = write_all(...)`. Fixed with `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)` as the first statement of `main`, via a direct `extern \"C\"` declaration so no new dependency was needed.\n\nI confirmed the regression test is load-bearing by **mutation**: commenting out the fix makes `stdout_pipe_with_dead_reader_matches` fail with exactly that diff; restoring it passes.\n\nEverything else matched on the first run — but I verified rather than assumed the tricky parts, notably that `scanf` reads across newlines (`\"\\n\\n\\n7\\n\"` → `07000000`), and that overflow **saturates in `long` then truncates** rather than wrapping in 32 bits. That produces C behavior worth calling out: `-99999999999999999999` prints `00000000`, identical to an input of `0`, while `99999999999999999999` prints `ffffffff`.\n\n## Phase D — gate\n\n- ✅ Both build clean (verified after `rm -rf c_src/build`)\n- ✅ `cargo test`: **16 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored** (~250 inputs: empty, whitespace-only, single item, `INT_MAX`/`INT_MIN`, `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` ±1, 5 000- and 10 000-digit strings, every matching-failure form, all 32 powers of two, exhaustive `-40..=40`)\n- ✅ Nothing `#[ignore]`d or disabled (`grep -c` → 0); no library/`libloading` usage — both binaries are driven as subprocesses comparing stdout, stderr, exit code **and** termination signal\n- ✅ `c_src/` unmodified (source mtimes unchanged; only the untracked `build/` dir was created, as instructed)\n\nAn additional throwaway fuzz sweep of 6 000 random inputs found 0 further mismatches. Details recorded in `translation/ERRORS.md`.\n\nOne caveat on scope: the two `#[cfg(unix)]` write-failure tests are Unix-only, and the overflow-saturation expectations encode **glibc**'s `strtol`-based `scanf`. On a different libc (e.g. musl) the saturation cases could legitimately differ, since C leaves that behavior unspecified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":897,"cache_read_input_tokens":43294,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":897,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ffa63926-e046-4a76-9f25-c0973763e042","uuid":"b9a3eca4-8525-4166-851d-b022a21de29a","timestamp":"2026-08-28T16:12:57.655Z"}
